Hi,

I have run into a scenario when using packrat::init() and thought I'd post it here in case it helps someone.

Also, while I understand what's going on, I wonder if there isn't a better way to handle this other than to write my code differently ;)  [[ this is always a solution to *any* programming problem ]]

Anyways. I have an old project & am trying to initialize packrat for this.

Here's what I see:

> packrat::init(options = list(local.repos = c("/analytics/local-cran/alpha")))
Initializing packrat project in directory:
- "/u/jburos/new_projects/analysis_explore_####"
Error in if (result$source == "unknown" && fallback.ok) { :
  missing value where TRUE/FALSE needed
In addition: Warning messages:
1: In value[[3L]](cond) :
  Failed to parse /u/jburos/new_projects/analysis_explore_###/examples/example_###_analysis.R ; dependencies in this file will not be discovered.
2: In FUN(X[[i]], ...) :
  Package 'x' not available in repository or locally
3: In FUN(X[[i]], ...) :
  Failed to infer source for package 'ggvis'; using latest available version on CRAN instead

Now, warning #2 was the first thing that caught my eye - package 'x' ? What is that?

Tried to grep by source files for usual suspects, but couldn't find this pernicious package 'x'.

So, I then did the following to figure out which file packrat thought was using :

files <- dir(pattern = '*.R$')
k <- list() ; for (file in files) {k[[file]] <- packrat:::fileDependencies.R(file)}

using this, I found the offending bit of code:
packages <- c('netezza','arm','blme','ggvis','stringr','plyr','dplyr','splines','reshape2','data.table','shiny','ggplot2','scales','gridExtra','directlabels','lmtest','memoise')
lapply(X = packages,FUN = function(x) suppressPackageStartupMessages(library(x,character.only = T, quietly = T, lib.loc = '/usr/lib64/R/library')))

Ah - so .. yikes. for now, I suspect I will have to rewrite this part for it to work with packrat. Posting this message here in case it's helpful to others.

In the long run, it would be nice to know which files are responsible for packrat's thinking a particular package is required by code.

Also, is there a way for me to (a) tell packrat which packages my project depends on, or (b) tell packrat to ignore package "x" ?

